# GENERATED from deploy/wz-config.toml by scripts/lib/wz_config.py --emit image-kconfig:zephyr-admin-node.
# Do not edit: `wz_config.py --check` fails when this file is stale.

choice WZ_NET_BACKEND
	prompt "Network stack the admin node runs over"
	default WZ_NET_BACKEND_ZEPHYR_SOCKETS
	help
	  The node is the same on every board; what differs is the stack under it,
	  which the board's Ethernet driver decides.

config WZ_NET_BACKEND_ZEPHYR_SOCKETS
	bool "Zephyr's own network stack and BSD sockets"
	help
	  For a board whose Ethernet, Wi-Fi or modem Zephyr drives. The interface, its
	  address (DHCP or Kconfig) and its link-layer identity are Zephyr's.

config WZ_NET_BACKEND_LWIP_MAC
	bool "lwIP over an Ethernet MAC the wz crates drive"
	depends on !WZ_ROLE_CLIENT
	help
	  For a board whose Ethernet controller Zephyr has no driver for. Zephyr is the
	  kernel only (threads, clock, heap, console): the network is lwIP, polled from
	  the node's loop, over a wz `EthernetMac` crate for the chip. The interface
	  address and the MAC address are the board's Kconfig, since no Zephyr
	  interface exists to ask.

endchoice

choice WZ_NODE_STORAGE
	prompt "Where the node's tables live"
	default WZ_STORAGE_HEAP
	help
	  The memory profile is the node's and not a network backend's: whether the Rust image has an
	  allocator is a setting made beside the role and the stack, and what it is available to is
	  what has the slot profile's pieces (the stack's driver and how it opens a socket).

config WZ_STORAGE_HEAP
	bool "The heap profile: the growable node, over an allocator in the Rust image"

config WZ_FIXED_MEMORY
	bool "Build the node on the fixed-memory profile (no allocator in the Rust image)"
	depends on (WZ_NET_BACKEND_LWIP_MAC && WZ_MAC_CYT4BF) || (WZ_NET_BACKEND_ZEPHYR_SOCKETS && WZ_ROLE_CLIENT)
	help
	  Every table the node holds (sessions, sockets, the observer, the interfaces) is a
	  static the image declares, the session tier is the slot profile's, and the Rust
	  staticlib is built with its default features off, so it links no allocator. The
	  session host and the dialler are the ones every stack shares
	  (wz_session_mcu::slot_host); a stack supplies its driver and how it opens a socket.
	
	  Two nodes have the profile. The admin node over lwIP is fixed_node.rs: its listener
	  reads only the datagrams of the peer each session was accepted from and drops and
	  counts the rest, where the growable node's one accepting session takes the source of
	  every datagram it reads as its peer. The client node over Zephyr's sockets is
	  fixed_client.rs. Not the admin node over Zephyr's sockets: that node accepts, and the
	  socket stack has no listener of the slot profile yet.
	
	  This kernel's own heap is a separate matter: what the Rust image does not allocate,
	  Zephyr's network stack and POSIX layer still may, and CONFIG_HEAP_MEM_POOL_SIZE is what
	  the kernel is given for it.

endchoice

choice WZ_LIFECYCLE_TRANSPORT
	prompt "How a session lifecycle verb reaches the node"
	default WZ_LIFECYCLE_IN_BAND
	help
	  The node holds the peers it was told to hold by their zid beside the locators it is told to
	  connect to, and which peers it is asked to hold at run time is a transport of its own: a Push or
	  a query on the admin space, an agent that drives the node over a channel of its own, or none.

config WZ_LIFECYCLE_IN_BAND
	bool "A query on the admin space opens a peer (session/open/<zid>)"
	help
	  The node answers `GET @/<zid>/<whatami>/session/open/<peer-zid>` by holding the peer and lists
	  the verb in `session/verbs`. Only the fixed-memory admin node binds the verb this way; the other
	  nodes of the image have their own lifecycle or none, and this value changes nothing of them.

config WZ_LIFECYCLE_DECLARED
	bool "No verb: the peers are the ones the build declares"
	depends on WZ_FIXED_MEMORY && WZ_NET_BACKEND_LWIP_MAC
	help
	  The node binds no lifecycle verb: its admin answer lists none and serves no `session/open` key, and
	  the code that serves it is not in the image. The peers it holds are the ones declared when it was
	  built, and its connection machines only reconnect them.

endchoice
